import{_ as r,o as t,c as a,a0 as o}from"./chunks/framework.BTg7EidG.js";const s="/athanor-docs/screens/command-bar.png",n="/athanor-docs/screens/hero-sheet.png",i="/athanor-docs/screens/dusk.png",h="/athanor-docs/screens/muster-rolls.png",l="/athanor-docs/screens/growth-sheet.png",d="/athanor-docs/screens/reinforce.png",b=JSON.parse('{"title":"Heroes & Parties","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"guide/heroes.md","filePath":"guide/heroes.md"}'),c={name:"guide/heroes.md"};function g(u,e,p,m,f,y){return t(),a("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[o('<h1 id="heroes-parties" tabindex="-1">Heroes &amp; Parties <a class="header-anchor" href="#heroes-parties" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Heroes &amp; Parties&quot;">​</a></h1><h2 id="the-command-bar" tabindex="-1">The command bar <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-command-bar" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The command bar&quot;">​</a></h2><p><img src="'+s+'" alt="The command bar"></p><p>Selecting a party raises the <strong>command bar</strong> across the bottom of the screen. Left to right:</p><ul><li><strong>The portrait plate</strong> carries your hero&#39;s vitals, movement, station, and travel status (with a <strong>Halt</strong> for standing orders). The <strong>✕</strong> deselects; tapping the hero opens their growth sheet.</li><li><strong>The retinue tray</strong> is always open: a card for every soldier with a health sliver and one badge for whatever matters most right now — <em>unpaid</em> (red), 💰 mercenary, ⚒ drilling, ✋ wage withheld, or 🎖 ready for promotion. Marchers on the road hold their slot with a green 🚩 and their arrival day. <strong>Tap a soldier to inspect them; hold to open the Muster board.</strong></li><li><strong>The command card</strong> keeps its slots in fixed places and greys them out of context: 🛡 Muster, 🚩 Reinforce, 🎖 Promote (lit and counted when someone qualifies), ⚔ Recruit, 🔭 Scout, 📜 Sheet (arms &amp; satchel), 🌾 farm work, and 🪤 beasts.</li></ul><h2 id="the-hero-sheet" tabindex="-1">The hero sheet <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-hero-sheet" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The hero sheet&quot;">​</a></h2><p><img src="'+n+'" alt="The hero sheet"></p><p>Every hero has <strong>two hand slots and three accessory slots</strong>. Weapons, shields and artifacts come from battle spoils, marauder hoards, and dungeon chests, and ride in the satchel of the party that found them. Tap an item there to equip it. The <strong>guild vault</strong> is another matter: it opens only inside one of your cities, so what one party finds in the far wilds reaches another party&#39;s hands by walking it home or sending a scout. Inside a city, <strong>Store all in vault</strong> on the hero sheet stows the party&#39;s finds (or hold a single item&#39;s chip to stow just that one); carried pieces are marked 🎒 and vaulted ones 🏛. A few tricks of the trade:</p><ul><li><strong>Warriors</strong> are strong enough to wield a two-handed weapon in <em>each</em> hand.</li><li><strong>Rogues</strong> shoot bows half again as hard, and a matched pair of daggers turns them into a whirlwind.</li></ul><p>Heroes also carry up to <strong>three skills</strong>. The class skill is innate; the rest are researched at a city&#39;s Town Hall and can then be assigned to any hero of the right class. The full catalog of arms lives in <a href="/athanor-docs/guide/artifacts.html">Arms &amp; Artifacts</a>, and every skill and its research path in <a href="/athanor-docs/guide/skills.html">Skills &amp; Research</a>.</p><h2 id="paying-the-army" tabindex="-1">Paying the army <a class="header-anchor" href="#paying-the-army" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Paying the army&quot;">​</a></h2><p><img src="'+i+'" alt="The dusk reckoning"></p><p>Every soldier under your banner draws a wage at dusk — <strong>heroes and garrisons included</strong>: 1⚗️ a warrior, 1🪙 a rogue, 1💎 an alchemist, 4🪙 a mercenary, and a creature&#39;s own keep. The purse pays out in a fixed order: heroes first (your Lord is always first in line), then anyone you have ⭐ pinned, then the field armies, then the walls. When coin runs short the tail of the line goes hungry — and every soldier keeps their own count. <strong>Three unpaid days and a regular deserts.</strong> A mercenary walks the first night pay is missed. An unpaid creature rampages through its own ranks and flies off. Your Lord grumbles, but stays.</p><p>The <strong>dusk chip</strong> in the top-right corner always shows tonight&#39;s bill and gains a ⚠ when someone will go short. Tap it for the <strong>dusk reckoning</strong>: the bill grouped by cause, how long each resource lasts (&quot;dry in 2 days&quot;), and <em>who walks first</em> — each with a one-tap out. And if ending the day would leave anyone unpaid, the reckoning opens by itself, so desertion is a choice you saw coming, never a surprise.</p><p>Two levers on the Muster Rolls put a shortfall under your control: <strong>⭐ pay first</strong> pins a soldier to the front of the line, and <strong>✋ withhold</strong> deliberately skips their wage — a wager against their three days of grace, for when the coffers need room to breathe. Heroes and mercenaries refuse to wait.</p><p>When a soldier has outlived their wage, <strong>disband</strong> them: half the bread and elixir that raised them returns to the stores (+20🍞 +10⚗️), though the Follower is spent. Mercenaries and other gold hires are dismissed with nothing — their pay stays spent.</p><h2 id="the-muster-rolls" tabindex="-1">The Muster Rolls <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-muster-rolls" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The Muster Rolls&quot;">​</a></h2><p><img src="'+h+'" alt="The Muster Rolls"></p><p>The 👥 button in the top bar opens the <strong>Muster Rolls</strong>: every soul under your banner — field armies, garrisons, marchers on the road (recallable from their row), even scouts away on errands — with level, strength, wage and standing side by side. Filters pick out the field, the walls, the mercenaries, or everyone <strong>at risk</strong>; copper rows are ready for promotion, red rows are in pay trouble; the ⭐/✋ toggles live on every row. The dark footer answers &quot;can I afford my army?&quot; at a glance and names the resource that runs dry first. Tap any row to open that soldier&#39;s growth sheet.</p><h2 id="growing-your-forces" tabindex="-1">Growing your forces <a class="header-anchor" href="#growing-your-forces" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Growing your forces&quot;">​</a></h2><p><img src="'+l+'" alt="The growth sheet"></p><p>Units level up from battle (experience for wounds dealt and kills) or paid <strong>training</strong>. Tap any soldier&#39;s card and their <strong>growth sheet</strong> lays the two roads side by side: earn the XP in the field (free, but they can die trying), or pay the drillmaster — who <strong>only charges for what is left to learn</strong>. A soldier three-quarters of the way to the next level trains for a quarter of the list price, so field experience and paid drilling work together. Training needs the soldier standing at one of your halls of their class, and the new level lands at day&#39;s end.</p><p>The sheet also carries every soldier&#39;s <strong>arms</strong>: tap a piece from the party&#39;s satchel to equip it on the spot, or tap a worn piece to stow it — no digging through menus. Any soldier can bear arms, not just heroes.</p><p>Recruiting takes a <strong>Follower</strong> plus food and elixir (the faithful march on bread, not coin), and every recruit signs on under a name of their own; <strong>mercenaries</strong> cost pure gold but demand pay every day and walk the moment you miss it. A veteran of the same class standing at the recruiting building mentors newcomers — recruits start at half the mentor&#39;s level.</p><h2 id="champions-and-more-heroes" tabindex="-1">Champions and more heroes <a class="header-anchor" href="#champions-and-more-heroes" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Champions and more heroes&quot;">​</a></h2><p>A veteran of <strong>level 3+</strong>, standing in one of your cities <strong>or at the hall that trains their class</strong> (a rogue at a Shadow Guild, a warrior at a Barracks, an alchemist at a Laboratory), can be <strong>promoted to Champion</strong> for 150🪙 — a hero who can lead a party of their own (every hero leads up to 8 units). The growth sheet shows the whole road as three checks — the level, the coin, the ground — with the button right there when all three pass; the command card&#39;s 🎖 slot lights up whenever anyone under the selected hero qualifies. Split champions off from the Muster board to run two armies at once. Rarely, a dungeon holds a <strong>caged hero</strong> who will join their rescuer — champion-ranked, but expensive to keep.</p><h2 id="the-muster-board-and-reinforcements" tabindex="-1">The Muster board and reinforcements <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-muster-board-and-reinforcements" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The Muster board and reinforcements&quot;">​</a></h2><p>At any of your buildings, the 🛡 <strong>Muster</strong> slot opens the board: every party on the tile and the garrison stand side by side as columns. <strong>Drag a soldier between columns</strong> — or onto the walls to station them — or tap a soldier for their orders. Every chip carries its daily wage, and the eligible carry their promotion button inline. Stationed units defend the walls (and mentor recruits at trainer buildings) until a hero fetches them back.</p><p><img src="'+d+'" alt="The Reinforce screen"></p><p><strong>Reinforce</strong> musters a recruit by class — the board lists what you can raise, notes the holdings it draws from, and picks your best source (strongest mentor first, then nearest) — and the recruit marches cross-country to the hero. The receiving party <strong>holds position until every marcher arrives</strong>. The 8-slot cap counts marchers on the road, contested holdings (under half their land held) cannot muster at all, and you can recall a marcher home to move early.</p><h2 id="scouts" tabindex="-1">Scouts <a class="header-anchor" href="#scouts" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Scouts&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Any of your cities will hire a <strong>Scout</strong> into the party standing there: fast, frail, and worth every coin. Send one out from the command card&#39;s 🔭 slot and choose their errand:</p><ul><li><strong>Chart the unknown</strong>: the scout rides for unexplored country, burning off the fog of war in a wide circle as they go, then turns for home.</li><li><strong>Carry the finds home</strong>: the scout loads up everything in the party&#39;s satchel and delivers it to the vault of your nearest city.</li></ul><p>Either way the party keeps marching; it never waits on a scout. The return leg chases wherever the party has gone since, so a three-day errand becomes a longer one if you have marched two days the other way. A scout in the party also slows it no more than any rogue, but mind that they are poor fighters when the line forms.</p>',34)])])}const v=r(c,[["render",g]]);export{b as __pageData,v as default};
